1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:11,780
This is Doing and Feeling, the Long Version. Episode 11, Theories on Trial. In 1998, the neuroscientist Christoph Koch made a bet with the philosopher David Chalmers.

2
00:00:12,100 --> 00:00:22,380
Koch wagered a case of fine wine that within 25 years, science would clearly identify the brain mechanism behind consciousness. Chalmers bet against.

3
00:00:22,380 --> 00:00:25,300
25 years later, in June 2023,

4
00:00:25,940 --> 00:00:31,140
at the annual meeting of the Association for the Scientific Study of Consciousness in New York,

5
00:00:31,660 --> 00:00:35,040
Koch conceded on stage and handed over a case of Madera.

6
00:00:35,660 --> 00:00:39,940
Then the two of them renewed the bet, with a new deadline of 2048.

7
00:00:40,340 --> 00:00:42,600
The concession came at the same session,

8
00:00:42,820 --> 00:00:47,760
where the first results were presented from an experiment pitting two leading theories against each other,

9
00:00:47,960 --> 00:00:51,820
with their own champions agreeing in advance on what would count as failure.

10
00:00:52,380 --> 00:00:53,880
We'll end with that experiment.

11
00:00:54,360 --> 00:00:58,180
First, the theories and a problem with how they've been tested.

12
00:00:59,200 --> 00:01:01,660
What should a theory of consciousness explain?

13
00:01:01,960 --> 00:01:04,700
Why some contents are conscious and others are not?

14
00:01:04,920 --> 00:01:07,600
Why we're conscious at some times and not others?

15
00:01:07,920 --> 00:01:11,060
Why experiences have the specific character they do?

16
00:01:11,280 --> 00:01:13,140
Why consciousness is unified?

17
00:01:13,520 --> 00:01:17,220
And what it adds to an organism that processes information anyway?

18
00:01:17,700 --> 00:01:19,660
No theory explains all five.

19
00:01:19,660 --> 00:01:28,680
A 2022 review by Anil Seth and Tim Bain listed 22 theories in circulation and examined four families in depth.

20
00:01:29,330 --> 00:01:33,920
Seth has also argued for a strategy he calls addressing the real problem.

21
00:01:34,080 --> 00:01:40,890
Rather than solving or dissolving the hard problem head-on, explain, predict, and control the properties of experience,

22
00:01:41,360 --> 00:01:44,100
the way biology dissolved the mystery of life.

23
00:01:44,100 --> 00:01:50,940
not by finding a vital spark, but by explaining metabolism, reproduction, and heredity one at a

24
00:01:50,940 --> 00:01:56,400
time. Whether consciousness will yield the same way is exactly what the hard problem of episode

25
00:01:56,400 --> 00:02:01,920
seven disputes. But the strategy has made the field productive. Where do researchers themselves

26
00:02:01,920 --> 00:02:09,560
stand? A survey of 166 attendees of the field's main conference in 2018 and 2019, published in

27
00:02:09,560 --> 00:02:16,620
2022, gives a snapshot. Predictive processing was rated promising by about 61% of respondents,

28
00:02:17,060 --> 00:02:23,320
global workspace theory by 58, higher order theories by 54, integrated information theory

29
00:02:23,320 --> 00:02:29,820
by 45, and recurrent processing by 39. Quantum theories drew the most votes as not promising.

30
00:02:30,320 --> 00:02:36,400
About two-thirds said machines could be conscious, now, or in the future. It was a self-selected

31
00:02:36,400 --> 00:02:43,220
sample, but it tells you the field has favorites and no consensus. Before the theories, a problem

32
00:02:43,220 --> 00:02:50,320
with the evidence for them. In 2022, Irei Yarin, Lucia Meloni, Michael Pitts, and Lyad Mudrick

33
00:02:50,320 --> 00:02:56,140
built a database of 412 experiments that interpreted their results in light of one

34
00:02:56,140 --> 00:03:01,640
or more of the four leading theories. They found that whether a study ended up supporting a given

35
00:03:01,640 --> 00:03:07,040
theory could be predicted from its methodological choices alone, before you looked at the results.

36
00:03:07,260 --> 00:03:12,820
In other words, the kind of experiment a lab chose strongly predicted which theory it would

37
00:03:12,820 --> 00:03:18,260
find support for. They also found that most studies interpreted their findings after the fact,

38
00:03:18,440 --> 00:03:22,060
rather than testing a theory's critical predictions in advance.

39
00:03:22,400 --> 00:03:27,740
So the field has been running the garden of forking paths at the level of whole paradigms.

40
00:03:27,900 --> 00:03:31,400
Each camp picks the experiments its theory is good at.

41
00:03:31,640 --> 00:03:37,600
That's the uncharitable version, and there's truth in it, though nobody needs to be acting in bad faith.

42
00:03:38,140 --> 00:03:42,500
Theories grow up around the phenomena that inspired them, and labs specialize.

43
00:03:42,840 --> 00:03:46,440
But piles of confirming studies tell you less than they seem to,

44
00:03:46,740 --> 00:03:52,420
which is the reason for the field's most important development this decade, adversarial collaborations.

45
00:03:52,800 --> 00:03:56,680
The Templeton World Charity Foundation has funded a series of them.

46
00:03:57,400 --> 00:04:02,920
Proponents of rival theories agree in advance on experiments whose outcomes would embarrass at least one of them,

47
00:04:03,120 --> 00:04:05,200
and neutral teams run the experiments.

48
00:04:05,500 --> 00:04:11,720
The program has pitted integrated information theory against global neuronal workspace theory, twice.

49
00:04:12,280 --> 00:04:18,240
First-order theories against higher-order ones, and integrated information theory against predictive processing.

50
00:04:18,540 --> 00:04:24,260
One workshop tried to design a test pitting integrated information theory against ORCH-OR.

51
00:04:24,260 --> 00:04:29,800
are the quantum theory from episode 9 and concluded that it could not find an experiment

52
00:04:29,800 --> 00:04:35,440
suitable for a head-to-head test, which is informative in itself. Keep that in mind as

53
00:04:35,440 --> 00:04:40,780
we go through the theories. What each claims, its best evidence, its best counter-evidence,

54
00:04:40,920 --> 00:04:46,780
and what it implies for machines. First, global workspace theory. Bernard

55
00:04:46,780 --> 00:04:52,600
Bars proposed it in 1988 with a theater metaphor. Most of what the brain does happens backstage,

56
00:05:03,000 --> 00:05:05,800
Thank you.

57
00:05:24,040 --> 00:05:30,740
cortex. Its signature event is ignition. When a stimulus crosses into awareness, activity does not

58
00:05:30,740 --> 00:05:36,660
just grow. It suddenly amplifies and spreads across the network, all or nothing, typically a few

59
00:05:36,660 --> 00:05:42,540
hundred milliseconds after the stimulus. DeHaan describes four markers, a late brainwave called

60
00:05:42,540 --> 00:05:48,740
the P3B, a sudden nonlinear amplification of activity, bursts of high-frequency oscillation,

61
00:05:48,740 --> 00:05:55,020
and synchronization between distant regions. In 2018, Bram van Vucket, Piotr Rulfsema,

62
00:05:55,200 --> 00:06:00,580
and colleagues recorded from monkeys detecting faint stimuli. When the monkey reported seeing

63
00:06:00,580 --> 00:06:05,640
the stimulus, activity propagated up to prefrontal cortex and sustained itself there,

64
00:06:05,820 --> 00:06:12,340
all or none. When it missed, the signal was lost along the way. And higher baseline activity in

65
00:06:12,340 --> 00:06:18,240
prefrontal cortex predicted false alarms. The theory explains the limited capacity of awareness,

66
00:06:18,740 --> 00:06:23,520
and why consciously seen information is available for flexible, multi-step reasoning,

67
00:06:23,780 --> 00:06:29,300
while unseen information is not. It's also the theory most naturally built into machines,

68
00:06:29,600 --> 00:06:34,740
because broadcast through a shared, limited-capacity medium is something an engineer can

69
00:06:34,740 --> 00:06:40,700
design. The trouble comes from the report Confound of Last Episode. In Inattentional

70
00:06:40,700 --> 00:06:45,920
Blindness Experiments by Michael Pitts and colleagues, the P3B appeared when stimuli

71
00:06:45,920 --> 00:06:51,680
were relevant to the task, but largely vanished when people saw the same stimuli without needing

72
00:06:51,680 --> 00:06:57,040
to report them. An earlier signal over the back of the brain, at around 200 to 300 milliseconds,

73
00:06:57,580 --> 00:07:03,160
called the visual awareness negativity, tracked awareness more faithfully. That suggests the

74
00:07:03,160 --> 00:07:08,280
classic marker reflects what you do with an experience rather than the experience itself.

75
00:07:08,840 --> 00:07:14,440
Workspace theorists reply that ignition need not involve report, and the argument continues.

76
00:07:15,920 --> 00:07:21,300
Second, higher-order theories. On these views, a mental state is conscious when you are,

77
00:07:21,440 --> 00:07:27,440
in a suitable way, aware of being in it. The philosopher David Rosenthal developed the higher-order

78
00:07:27,440 --> 00:07:32,480
thought version. A perception becomes conscious when it's accompanied by a thought that you are

79
00:07:32,480 --> 00:07:38,160
having it. The classic objection concerns empty higher-order states. Suppose the higher-order

80
00:07:38,160 --> 00:07:43,160
thought says you're seeing red, but there's no red perception underneath at all. What is it like to

81
00:07:43,160 --> 00:07:49,320
be you? Rosenthal bites the bullet. You'd seem to see red, so you'd be conscious of red. Critics

82
00:07:49,320 --> 00:07:54,140
like Ned Block reply that the theory has now located experience in the higher-order state,

83
00:07:54,460 --> 00:07:59,860
leaving the perception idle. The most influential current version is Hakwin Lau's perceptual

84
00:07:59,860 --> 00:08:05,920
reality monitoring theory. Sensory systems produce activity all the time, from real inputs,

85
00:08:06,200 --> 00:08:12,440
from imagination, from memory, from noise. Consciousness, on Lau's view, arises when a

86
00:08:13,160 --> 00:08:19,060
mechanism tags a sensory representation as a reliable reflection of the world, right now.

87
00:08:19,600 --> 00:08:25,480
Lau has compared this to a generative adversarial network, in which a discriminator learns to tell

88
00:08:25,480 --> 00:08:30,880
real data from generated data. That gives you a mechanism for hallucination for free.

89
00:08:31,260 --> 00:08:37,980
The discriminator misfires and tags imagination as real. And Lau uses it exactly that way.

90
00:08:37,980 --> 00:08:41,340
hallucinations as failures of reality monitoring

91
00:08:41,340 --> 00:08:43,900
and blindsight as the opposite failure,

92
00:08:44,180 --> 00:08:46,260
where real signals do not get tagged.

93
00:08:46,700 --> 00:08:49,600
The evidence for this family includes experiments

94
00:08:49,600 --> 00:08:52,220
in which people perform equally well on a task

95
00:08:52,220 --> 00:08:55,360
but differ in how aware or confident they feel

96
00:08:55,360 --> 00:08:58,180
with matching differences in prefrontal activity.

97
00:08:58,440 --> 00:09:00,920
There's a cautionary tale from this camp, too.

98
00:09:01,300 --> 00:09:05,080
A 2010 study found that magnetic stimulation of prefrontal cortex

99
00:09:05,080 --> 00:09:10,000
reduced people's ability to judge their own accuracy without impairing the task itself,

100
00:09:10,200 --> 00:09:16,360
and it was widely cited as support. In 2017, Daniel Bohr and colleagues tried to reproduce

101
00:09:16,360 --> 00:09:22,500
it with 90 participants, then ran a follow-up and found no effect. The statistics favored the null.

102
00:09:22,920 --> 00:09:27,940
One more classic to downgrade. The main empirical objection to higher-order theories

103
00:09:27,940 --> 00:09:32,260
is that large prefrontal lesions do not seem to abolish consciousness.

104
00:09:32,960 --> 00:09:38,540
In 2017, the Journal of Neuroscience published a paired debate on precisely this.

105
00:09:38,780 --> 00:09:42,860
One team, including Melanie Boley, Koch, and Giulio Tononi,

106
00:09:43,360 --> 00:09:47,360
argued that the neural correlates of consciousness lie mainly in the back of the brain.

107
00:09:47,740 --> 00:09:50,980
The other, Brian Odegaard, Robert Knight, and Lau,

108
00:09:51,480 --> 00:09:53,640
argued that prefrontal cortex is essential

109
00:09:53,640 --> 00:09:56,560
and that the negative findings were weaker than claimed.

110
00:09:56,940 --> 00:10:00,620
That front-versus-back fight runs through the rest of this episode.

111
00:10:02,260 --> 00:10:05,120
Third, recurrent processing theory from Victor Lama.

112
00:10:05,320 --> 00:10:07,340
When a visual stimulus arrives,

113
00:10:07,600 --> 00:10:12,520
a fast feed-forward sweep carries it up the visual hierarchy in about a tenth of a second,

114
00:10:12,880 --> 00:10:16,620
enough to drive quick responses, but on this view unconscious.

115
00:10:17,120 --> 00:10:19,100
Then feedback connections kick in,

116
00:10:19,540 --> 00:10:22,560
creating local recurrent processing within sensory cortex.

117
00:10:23,060 --> 00:10:27,660
Lama argues that this local recurrence is sufficient for phenomenal experience.

118
00:10:28,210 --> 00:10:33,920
wider recurrence involving frontal areas adds access, report, memory, and control.

119
00:10:34,180 --> 00:10:40,060
The evidence includes the timing of masking, which seems to work by interrupting recurrent processing,

120
00:10:40,320 --> 00:10:44,600
and experiments in which a magnetic pulse over primary visual cortex,

121
00:10:44,860 --> 00:10:47,920
delivered roughly a tenth of a second after a stimulus,

122
00:10:48,160 --> 00:10:51,820
when feedback should be arriving, can abolish awareness of it.

123
00:10:52,040 --> 00:10:57,140
Recurrent processing theory is the natural ally of Bloch's overflow view from Episode 7,

124
00:10:57,660 --> 00:11:01,100
Experience in local loops, access for only some of it.

125
00:11:01,460 --> 00:11:04,200
Global workspace theory sits on the other side.

126
00:11:04,440 --> 00:11:06,480
No access, no experience.

127
00:11:06,940 --> 00:11:10,300
Its main vulnerability is the refrigerator light problem.

128
00:11:10,620 --> 00:11:14,680
If local recurrence produces experiences that never reach report,

129
00:11:14,960 --> 00:11:16,980
how will we ever confirm they exist?

130
00:11:17,400 --> 00:11:20,300
For machines, the theory has a clean implication.

131
00:11:20,700 --> 00:11:23,580
Purely feed-forward computation is not enough.

132
00:11:23,940 --> 00:11:25,040
Recurrence matters.

133
00:11:26,240 --> 00:11:31,240
Fourth, integrated information theory, whose philosophy we met in episode 9.

134
00:11:31,500 --> 00:11:32,820
Here is the science.

135
00:11:33,360 --> 00:11:38,800
IIT predicts that the physical substrate of experience lies mainly in a posterior hot

136
00:11:38,800 --> 00:11:41,960
zone of the cortex, rather than in prefrontal cortex.

137
00:11:42,500 --> 00:11:47,740
The dreaming studies from last episode fit that prediction, and the Zappan-Zipp complexity

138
00:11:47,740 --> 00:11:53,840
measure was inspired by IIT's idea that consciousness requires both integration and

139
00:11:53,840 --> 00:11:54,340
differentiation.

140
00:11:55,040 --> 00:11:58,080
It also explains a striking fact about the cerebellum,

141
00:11:58,240 --> 00:12:04,060
which contains about 69 billion of the brain's 86 billion neurons, 4 out of 5.

142
00:12:04,500 --> 00:12:09,180
Yet damage to it, even its complete absence in rare people born without one,

143
00:12:09,540 --> 00:12:13,920
affects movement, speech, and sometimes learning, while consciousness remains.

144
00:12:14,200 --> 00:12:19,440
On IIT's account, the cerebellum's highly modular, parallel architecture

145
00:12:19,440 --> 00:12:24,300
generates little integrated information, despite its huge neuron count.

146
00:12:25,040 --> 00:12:28,600
The skeptics of episode 13 lean on exactly this example.

147
00:12:29,260 --> 00:12:31,500
IIT makes stranger predictions, too.

148
00:12:31,820 --> 00:12:35,420
Because its measure, phi, depends on what a system could do,

149
00:12:35,720 --> 00:12:37,120
not only on what it's doing,

150
00:12:37,580 --> 00:12:42,460
IIT predicts that neurons which are inactive but could fire still contribute to experience,

151
00:12:42,840 --> 00:12:46,320
while the same neurons silenced pharmacologically would not.

152
00:12:46,740 --> 00:12:50,900
That's testable in principle, though nobody has yet done it convincingly.

153
00:12:51,100 --> 00:12:53,460
Now the criticisms, which are severe.

154
00:12:55,040 --> 00:13:00,100
computing phi exactly explodes with the number of elements, so it has only been computed for tiny

155
00:13:00,100 --> 00:13:06,540
systems, using software called PyPhi developed by Will Maynor and colleagues. In practice,

156
00:13:07,080 --> 00:13:12,920
every empirical test uses proxies. Then there's the unfolding argument from Adrian Doerig,

157
00:13:13,180 --> 00:13:19,680
Aaron Scherger, Michael Herzog, and colleagues in 2019. Any recurrent network can be unfolded

158
00:13:19,680 --> 00:13:24,800
into a purely feedforward network that computes exactly the same input-output function.

159
00:13:25,560 --> 00:13:30,960
IIT assigns substantial phi to the recurrent version and zero to the feedforward one,

160
00:13:31,140 --> 00:13:36,440
but every experiment we can run is read through outputs, so it would treat the two identically.

161
00:13:36,880 --> 00:13:42,260
Either IIT's claims about which system is conscious can never be tested, or they're false.

162
00:13:42,440 --> 00:13:47,380
And the same argument, its authors point out, threatens recurrent processing theory.

163
00:13:47,740 --> 00:13:52,160
That's devastating if you think consciousness must make a functional difference.

164
00:13:52,160 --> 00:13:55,000
two behaviorally identical systems,

165
00:13:55,000 --> 00:13:58,840
and the theory says one is conscious and the other is not.

166
00:13:59,100 --> 00:14:02,140
Which is exactly where IIT's defenders dig in.

167
00:14:02,820 --> 00:14:06,580
Consciousness, they argue, is about intrinsic causal structure,

168
00:14:06,840 --> 00:14:10,980
which perturbation experiments like ZAP and ZIP can probe directly

169
00:14:10,980 --> 00:14:13,060
beyond input-output behavior.

170
00:14:13,260 --> 00:14:17,140
And they argue that the critics are smuggling in a functionalist assumption.

171
00:14:17,460 --> 00:14:21,140
Whether that's a good reply depends on your answer to Episode 7.

172
00:14:22,160 --> 00:14:27,340
there's the logic gate grid from episode 9, which Tononi accepted rather than retreat.

173
00:14:27,700 --> 00:14:35,720
In September 2023, the tension boiled over. 124 researchers signed an open letter calling

174
00:14:35,720 --> 00:14:41,660
IIT pseudoscience, arguing that its core claims are untestable and that media coverage had

175
00:14:41,660 --> 00:14:47,660
overstated its support. Many researchers, including some who reject IIT, thought the

176
00:14:47,660 --> 00:14:54,320
label went much too far. In 2025, the fight moved into nature neuroscience, an expanded critique,

177
00:14:54,620 --> 00:15:00,740
a reply from Tone Oni and 21 co-authors arguing that the label reflected a bias toward computational

178
00:15:00,740 --> 00:15:07,500
functionalism, and a third piece by Alex Gomez-Marin and Anil Seth calling for the field to get past

179
00:15:07,500 --> 00:15:13,580
the name calling. The underlying question is a real one. Can a theory of consciousness be both

180
00:15:13,580 --> 00:15:20,600
scientific and non-functionalist. Fifth, the predictive processing family, which grows out of

181
00:15:20,600 --> 00:15:26,660
the predictive brain of episode 5. Anil Seth's version is the beast machine. If perception is

182
00:15:26,660 --> 00:15:31,060
the brain's best guess about the causes of its sensory signals, a controlled hallucination,

183
00:15:31,360 --> 00:15:36,860
then the deepest layer of prediction concerns the body itself. Heartbeat, blood pressure, gut,

184
00:15:37,160 --> 00:15:42,380
temperature, the signals called interosptive. The brain predicts and regulates these to keep

185
00:15:44,040 --> 00:15:50,440
Seth argues that emotions, moods, and the most basic feeling of being a self are rooted in that predictive regulation.

186
00:15:50,720 --> 00:15:55,380
We're conscious, on this view, not despite being animals, but because we are animals.

187
00:15:55,660 --> 00:15:59,180
There's suggestive evidence that bodily signals shape perception.

188
00:15:59,580 --> 00:16:05,740
In 2014, Hyondang Park and colleagues in Paris found that the brain's response to each heartbeat

189
00:16:05,740 --> 00:16:09,120
predicted whether people would detect a faint visual stimulus.

190
00:16:09,820 --> 00:16:14,120
Lisa Feldman Barrett's theory of constructed emotion makes a related claim.

191
00:16:14,560 --> 00:16:17,560
Emotions are predictions grounded in interosption.

192
00:16:18,000 --> 00:16:19,840
Mark Solms pushes further down.

193
00:16:20,060 --> 00:16:25,300
In The Hidden Spring, and in a 2019 paper on the hard problem and the free energy principle,

194
00:16:25,680 --> 00:16:30,000
he argues that consciousness originates not in the cortex but in the upper brainstem.

195
00:16:30,320 --> 00:16:37,760
It begins, he says, as raw feeling effect, which registers how well the organism is doing at staying alive.

196
00:16:39,120 --> 00:16:44,840
uncertainty felt as unpleasant, its resolution as pleasant. He draws on Jock Panksepp's work

197
00:16:44,840 --> 00:16:50,760
on emotional circuits deep in the brain, and on Bjorn Merker's observations of children born with

198
00:16:50,760 --> 00:16:56,920
little or no cerebral cortex, who nonetheless seem alert, emotionally responsive, and able to

199
00:16:56,920 --> 00:17:02,520
recognize the people they know. Merker's target article in Behavioral and Brain Sciences drew

200
00:17:02,520 --> 00:17:08,100
commentaries showing how contested this is. Some of these children retain islands of cortex,

201
00:17:09,120 --> 00:17:15,340
argue the behaviors fall short of establishing experience. Antonio Damasio, from Descartes

202
00:17:15,340 --> 00:17:21,300
Error Onward, has long argued that feeling is grounded in the body's homeostasis. On the other

203
00:17:21,300 --> 00:17:27,420
side, Joseph Ledoux and Richard Brown argue that emotional experiences are higher-order cortical

204
00:17:27,420 --> 00:17:32,800
states, and that the deep survival circuits produce defensive responses, not felt fear.

205
00:17:33,120 --> 00:17:38,860
Which side is right decides what we should say about fish, newborns, and patients with little

206
00:17:39,120 --> 00:17:47,920
And in 2025, Ruben Lachonen, Carl Friston, and Shamal Shandaria proposed an active inference theory called a beautiful loop.

207
00:17:48,100 --> 00:17:49,820
It names three conditions.

208
00:17:50,120 --> 00:17:54,960
A unified, simulated model of the world, which they call the epistemic field.

209
00:17:55,120 --> 00:18:02,140
A competition among inferences, in which those that best reduce long-term uncertainty win and are bound together.

210
00:18:02,680 --> 00:18:08,060
An epistemic depth, in which the model's beliefs are shared recurrently through its own hierarchy,

211
00:18:08,320 --> 00:18:11,400
so that the model contains knowledge of its own existence.

212
00:18:11,820 --> 00:18:12,800
That's the loop.

213
00:18:13,080 --> 00:18:17,320
The authors use the meditative states from last episode as test cases.

214
00:18:17,620 --> 00:18:19,720
What does this family imply for machines?

215
00:18:20,000 --> 00:18:21,860
It depends which member you ask.

216
00:18:22,080 --> 00:18:25,880
On Seth's beast machine view, prediction alone is not enough.

217
00:18:26,240 --> 00:18:29,080
Prediction in the service of staying alive is what matters,

218
00:18:29,080 --> 00:18:32,320
so today's disembodied systems are poor candidates.

219
00:18:32,820 --> 00:18:36,020
On a more purely computational reading of active inference,

220
00:18:36,260 --> 00:18:40,740
an artificial agent with the right self-modeling architecture could qualify.

221
00:18:41,960 --> 00:18:45,020
Sixth, Michael Graziano's attention schema theory,

222
00:18:45,280 --> 00:18:47,700
the illusionist's mechanism from episode 8.

223
00:18:47,900 --> 00:18:50,980
As a scientific theory, it makes a specific prediction.

224
00:18:51,360 --> 00:18:53,800
Awareness should matter for controlling attention.

225
00:18:54,060 --> 00:18:57,920
And that's roughly what experiments by Taylor Webb, Graziano,

226
00:18:59,080 --> 00:19:04,860
in 2016. Q's people were not aware of still captured their attention. But without awareness,

227
00:19:05,340 --> 00:19:08,720
people controlled their attention less well, holding it less stably.

228
00:19:09,580 --> 00:19:14,160
Transformers are literally built out of attention. Do they have attention schemas?

229
00:19:14,500 --> 00:19:21,340
Not in Graziano's sense. Attention in a transformer is a routing mechanism. Each position

230
00:19:21,340 --> 00:19:26,800
weighs information from the others. It does not come with a model of that attention used to control

231
00:19:26,800 --> 00:19:32,260
it. Whether something like that could be learned implicitly is an open question, and it's one of

232
00:19:32,260 --> 00:19:39,400
the indicators researchers check for, as we'll see next episode. Seventh, unlimited associative

233
00:19:39,400 --> 00:19:46,400
learning from Simona Ginsberg and Eva Yablonka. Rather than a mechanism, it identifies a transition

234
00:19:46,400 --> 00:19:51,460
marker, an evolved capacity whose presence indicates that consciousness has arisen.

235
00:19:51,460 --> 00:19:57,680
Unlimited associative learning is open-ended learning about novel, compound stimuli and actions,

236
00:19:57,940 --> 00:20:01,620
combinations of features, across time gaps, in chains.

237
00:20:02,220 --> 00:20:08,060
On their account, it first evolved around the Cambrian Explosion more than 500 million years ago,

238
00:20:08,100 --> 00:20:14,200
and consciousness evolved at least three times, in vertebrates, in arthropods, and in cephalopods.

239
00:20:14,580 --> 00:20:19,920
Language models learn associations open-endedly. By that marker, they are in.

240
00:20:21,460 --> 00:20:27,740
Yablonka would say no. In a 2021 paper with Zohar Bronfman on when robots might be sentient,

241
00:20:27,960 --> 00:20:33,300
they argued that a machine would need this kind of learning tied to a body and to one overriding

242
00:20:33,300 --> 00:20:39,600
value, keeping itself going. Language models have neither. And that shows the general problem with

243
00:20:39,600 --> 00:20:46,600
transplanting any marker calibrated on animals. A few other theories deserve a mention. Dendritic

244
00:20:46,600 --> 00:20:52,980
integration theory, from Yan Aru, Matto Takasuzuki, and Matthew Larkham, ties consciousness to a

245
00:20:52,980 --> 00:20:59,100
cellular mechanism in large cortical neurons, and in rodents, anesthetics appear to work in part by

246
00:20:59,100 --> 00:21:05,100
disrupting it. Serial Penarts's neurorepresentationalism treats consciousness as the

247
00:21:05,100 --> 00:21:10,300
brain's multimodal, situational model of the world. And electromagnetic field theories,

248
00:21:10,700 --> 00:21:16,520
like John Joe McFadden's, locate the integration of experience in the brain's own electric field.

249
00:21:16,600 --> 00:21:23,420
The skeptics of episode 13 cite recent work from Earl Miller's lab suggesting that the brain's

250
00:21:23,420 --> 00:21:29,300
electric fields help coordinate activity across the cortex, as one-way biology might matter.

251
00:21:30,140 --> 00:21:36,960
Now the trials. The first, and so far the most important, was the Cogitate Consortium's test

252
00:21:36,960 --> 00:21:43,880
of global neuronal workspace theory against integrated information theory. 256 participants

253
00:21:43,880 --> 00:21:47,640
were tested with three methods, FMRIM,

254
00:21:47,960 --> 00:21:50,860
e.g., and electrodes implanted in the brains

255
00:21:50,860 --> 00:21:52,700
of 34 epilepsy patients.

256
00:21:52,960 --> 00:21:56,980
They saw faces, objects, letters, and meaningless symbols

257
00:21:56,980 --> 00:21:59,580
at different orientations and durations,

258
00:21:59,880 --> 00:22:02,920
sometimes relevant to their task and sometimes not.

259
00:22:03,340 --> 00:22:05,360
The theories made opposing predictions.

260
00:22:06,160 --> 00:22:08,160
IIT predicted that conscious content

261
00:22:08,160 --> 00:22:10,120
would be maintained in the back of the brain

262
00:22:10,120 --> 00:22:12,260
for as long as the stimulus was seen,

263
00:22:13,940 --> 00:22:20,160
between posterior regions. Global workspace theory predicted ignition in prefrontal cortex

264
00:22:20,160 --> 00:22:25,380
when the stimulus appeared and again when it disappeared, and that prefrontal cortex would

265
00:22:25,380 --> 00:22:31,980
carry the details of what was seen. The results, published in Nature in April 2025, challenged both.

266
00:22:32,300 --> 00:22:37,360
Some predictions held up. Some sites at the back tracked how long a stimulus lasted,

267
00:22:37,640 --> 00:22:43,600
and prefrontal cortex responded when it appeared. But key predictions failed. There was no sustained,

268
00:22:43,880 --> 00:22:46,980
content-specific synchronization within posterior cortex,

269
00:22:47,400 --> 00:22:50,520
which the authors called the most direct challenge to IIT,

270
00:22:50,800 --> 00:22:53,520
and there was no ignition when the stimulus disappeared.

271
00:22:54,060 --> 00:22:58,120
Prefrontal cortex carried information about the category of what was seen,

272
00:22:58,400 --> 00:23:01,980
face versus object, but not about its exact identity

273
00:23:01,980 --> 00:23:04,460
and little or nothing about its orientation.

274
00:23:05,060 --> 00:23:08,180
That's a serious problem for global workspace theory.

275
00:23:08,660 --> 00:23:11,580
So the first decisive test was indecisive.

276
00:23:12,280 --> 00:23:18,000
Decisive about some predictions, indecisive about the theories, which is how science usually works.

277
00:23:18,360 --> 00:23:21,120
Both camps disputed parts of the interpretation,

278
00:23:21,460 --> 00:23:25,740
and the pseudoscience letter erupted a few months after the first results were presented.

279
00:23:26,100 --> 00:23:28,400
But the method is the real result.

280
00:23:28,780 --> 00:23:33,820
Theories made risky, pre-registered predictions, and some of those predictions failed.

281
00:23:34,120 --> 00:23:35,620
More rounds are underway.

282
00:23:36,380 --> 00:23:38,860
Cogitate's second experiment uses a video game.

283
00:23:39,360 --> 00:23:42,460
People play while faces and objects appear in the background,

284
00:23:42,740 --> 00:23:44,740
so their attention is pulled away from them,

285
00:23:44,940 --> 00:23:48,400
and later watch replays of gameplay while attending to those images.

286
00:23:48,840 --> 00:23:53,160
It was introduced in posters at the field's 2025 meeting in Crete,

287
00:23:53,360 --> 00:23:58,560
with 139 participants across FMR, I&M, EG.

288
00:23:58,820 --> 00:24:02,600
Those posters showed that the game works and gave early mixed results,

289
00:24:02,940 --> 00:24:05,120
weak support for a key workspace prediction,

290
00:24:05,360 --> 00:24:07,900
and only partial support for one of IITs.

291
00:24:08,860 --> 00:24:14,180
is peer-reviewed yet, and no verdict on the theories has been published. A separate collaboration,

292
00:24:14,500 --> 00:24:20,320
called Intrepid, is testing IIT against active inference and against neurorepresentationalism,

293
00:24:20,540 --> 00:24:25,780
with four experiments and independent replications planned. Its results are pending, too.

294
00:24:26,780 --> 00:24:32,100
What would settle things? A few candidate tests. IIT's inactive neuron prediction,

295
00:24:32,380 --> 00:24:37,880
if anyone can make it work. Lesion and stimulation studies of prefrontal cortex that are large enough

296
00:24:38,860 --> 00:24:40,840
to settle the front-versus-back fight.

297
00:24:41,040 --> 00:24:44,720
No report designs that cleanly separate experience from access.

298
00:24:45,020 --> 00:24:48,300
And the extreme states of last episode, cessations,

299
00:24:48,760 --> 00:24:51,340
keryemine dreams, awareness without content,

300
00:24:51,600 --> 00:24:53,540
which any complete theory has to handle.

301
00:24:53,980 --> 00:24:56,900
Meanwhile, let me line the theories up against the question

302
00:24:56,900 --> 00:24:58,820
this series is building toward.

303
00:24:59,300 --> 00:25:02,520
Global workspace theory says a machine could be conscious

304
00:25:02,520 --> 00:25:04,980
if it has a limited-capacity workspace

305
00:25:04,980 --> 00:25:08,660
whose contents are broadcast to many specialized processes.

306
00:25:09,410 --> 00:25:14,840
Higher-order theories say yes if the machine monitors its own representations in the right way.

307
00:25:15,080 --> 00:25:18,640
Recurrent processing theory requires genuine recurrence.

308
00:25:19,360 --> 00:25:24,840
Attention schema theory requires a model of the machine's own attention used to control it.

309
00:25:25,460 --> 00:25:30,900
Integrated information theory says no for conventional digital hardware, whatever the software.

310
00:25:31,140 --> 00:25:38,720
The beast machine version of predictive processing makes systems that are not regulating a living body very unlikely candidates.

311
00:25:39,420 --> 00:25:45,620
And unlimited associative learning, on its author's account, would require open-ended learning in a body,

312
00:25:45,880 --> 00:25:50,160
driven by something like self-preservation, which today's systems lack.

313
00:25:50,460 --> 00:25:56,660
So whether today's AI systems are candidates for consciousness depends almost entirely on which theory you believe,

314
00:25:56,840 --> 00:25:58,200
and no theory has won.

315
00:25:58,380 --> 00:26:00,380
That is not a reason for despair.

316
00:26:00,580 --> 00:26:04,500
It's the starting point for the most careful attempts yet to assess machines.

317
00:26:05,090 --> 00:26:10,900
In 2023, a group of 19 researchers turned most of these theories, though not IIT,

318
00:26:11,440 --> 00:26:15,560
into a checklist of architectural indicators that you can look for in an AI system.

319
00:26:15,900 --> 00:26:17,520
That's where Part 3 begins.

